1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,680
 Hey everyone, Ryan here, and welcome back to our head and neck anatomy series of videos.

2
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:11,040
 In this video, we're going to be talking about Facial Space Infections.

3
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:14,680
 And this is an incredibly, incredibly important topic.

4
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:17,960
 I'm actually really excited about this presentation.

5
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:20,840
 It has a lot of clinical relevance.

6
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:24,820
 It appears on all sorts of board exams.

7
00:00:24,820 --> 00:00:27,840
 And it also fits in really nicely with head and neck anatomy.

8
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:32,280
 I've wanted to do this topic for a while now, and I feel like it really fits in well with

9
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:34,800
 this content.

10
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:37,960
 So first, let's talk about what the fascia is.

11
00:00:37,960 --> 00:00:43,860
 F fascia is thin casing of connective tissue beneath the skin that surrounds and holds

12
00:00:43,860 --> 00:00:48,480
 every organ, blood vessel, bone, nerve fiber, and muscle in place.

13
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:54,120
 If you've ever done a cadaver dissection in gross anatomy lab, this is all that stuff

14
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:58,860
 that you're peeling through to get to your nerves and your blood vessels.

15
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:03,880
 It's a lot of the dissecting time is in and through the fascia layers.

16
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:05,760
 There are three distinct layers.

17
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:12,040
 The superficial fascia is a loose connective tissue directly, immediately under the dermis

18
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:13,360
 layer.

19
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:18,040
 The deep fascia is dense connective tissue that's deeper down and surrounds the actual

20
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:21,120
 muscles, bones, nerves, and blood vessels.

21
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:26,480
 And the visceral fascia is that specific layer that suspends organs like the heart and the

22
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:30,520
 lungs within their respective cavities.

23
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:34,520
 So since we're talking about head and neck, let's talk about the superficial cervical

24
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:37,920
 fascia first, fascia within the neck.

25
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:45,080
 It's again located beneath the dermis, between the dermis and the deep cervical fascia.

26
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:47,200
 And it surrounds the platysma muscle.

27
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:52,560
 This is a muscle of facial expression located within your neck, as well as the superficial

28
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:55,160
 vessels and nerves that run there.

29
00:01:55,160 --> 00:02:02,560
 The deep cervical fascia is a little bit more complex, and there are four or five specific

30
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:05,400
 layers that we're going to talk about.

31
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:14,000
 The investing layer encloses the sternocleidomastoid muscle and trapezius muscles.

32
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,520
 The sternocleidomastoid is out in front anteriorly.

33
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:22,120
 The trapezius muscles are located posteriorly.

34
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:27,920
 The visceral fascia layer surrounds the viscera of the neck.

35
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:35,040
 And so all of those neck organs are being surrounded by this tissue.

36
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:39,200
 The trachea, the esophagus are also located within there.

37
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:44,560
 And so the visceral layer, we can break down further into these two distinct layers.

38
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:51,520
 Pre-tracheal, which is in light blue, that's anterior and located in front of the trachea.

39
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:58,200
 And the bucco-fringiolayer is this light green layer that runs posteriorly to close everything

40
00:02:58,200 --> 00:02:59,880
 up there.

41
00:02:59,880 --> 00:03:06,720
 The pre-vertebral fascia encloses the vertebral region of the neck, surrounding the vertebral

42
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:12,920
 muscles, the cervical plexus, the brachial plexus, as well as the actual vertebral column.

43
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:16,440
 That's this orange layer here.

44
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:18,880
 And then we have the carotid sheath.

45
00:03:18,880 --> 00:03:24,300
 This is this brown circular layer on one on either side.

46
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:29,520
 And it surrounds some really important things.

47
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:34,760
 Encloses the vascular region of the neck, which includes the common carotid artery, the

48
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:41,800
 internal jugular vein, the vagus nerve, or cranial nerve, ten, and the deep cervical

49
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:43,440
 lymph nodes.

50
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:48,160
 All four of those contents are incredibly important to memorize.

51
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:54,600
 So the carotid sheath, one on either side, is enclosing all four of those things.

52
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:57,880
 So that is the deep cervical fascia.

53
00:03:57,880 --> 00:04:05,480
 Okay, so now we can talk about fascal spaces, so that deep fascia within the neck, as well

54
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:13,960
 as the head, enclose potential spaces into which infections of dental origin can spread.

55
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:20,380
 Now as a general rule of thumb, infection will always follow the path of least resistance.

56
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:27,440
 So the easiest path for bacteria and their byproducts to travel, by and large, they will

57
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:28,520
 do that.

58
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:32,000
 So this depends on bone density, muscle attachment.

59
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,060
 So if you have really dense bone, infection is going to have a much harder time of getting

60
00:04:36,060 --> 00:04:37,060
 through there.

61
00:04:37,060 --> 00:04:42,120
 If you have a thick muscle attachment, like the masseter that's attaching to that angle

62
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:46,120
 of the ramus, the angle of the mandible, it's going to be very hard for infection to get

63
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:48,960
 past a muscular attachment.

64
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:53,840
 So things like that are important to consider as we move ahead.

65
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:56,480
 Some cardinal signs of infection.

66
00:04:56,480 --> 00:05:02,120
 These are pretty classic signs that are, again, another really good thing to kind of lock

67
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:09,720
 in the back of your head, doleur means pain, tumor means swelling, calor means heat, rubour

68
00:05:09,720 --> 00:05:14,360
 means redness, and function olesa means loss of function.

69
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:22,800
 So this is referring to things like trismus, which is a lock jaw, dyspnea, difficulty breathing,

70
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:28,640
 phagia, difficulty swallowing, dysponia, difficulty talking, and things like that.

71
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:35,160
 So lots of function loss can be attributed to a spread of infection.

72
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:40,960
 These first four are probably things that might sound familiar from previous lectures

73
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:42,600
 that we've done.

74
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:46,680
 Some vital signs of infection, if we're talking about the integrated board exam, these are

75
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:52,240
 things that might pop up within that patient box, that all important patient box.

76
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:58,800
 So a fever, high temperature, elevated blood pressure, elevated pulse, elevated respiratory

77
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:06,600
 rate, and lymphadenopathy are all vital signs that might be pointing to some infection.

78
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:16,160
 All right, so primary spaces, primary-facial spaces, are those that can be infected via

79
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:22,080
 direct spread from periapical or periodontal infection.

80
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:28,720
 So adanogenic infection can go straight to one of these six primary spaces.

81
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:33,680
 So we're going to go through these one at a time, canine, buckle, submandibular, submental,

82
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:34,680
 and sublingual.

83
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:42,360
 These three are referred to as the peri-mandibular spaces, as well as the vestibular space.

84
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:44,920
 Then of course, we also have secondary spaces.

85
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:51,800
 These are those that are infected via spread from another fascial space.

86
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:57,320
 So another space has to be infected first, either one of these primary spaces, or maybe

87
00:06:57,320 --> 00:06:59,520
 even one of these secondary spaces.

88
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:04,600
 And then that space infects another space, which is considered a secondary space.

89
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:11,240
 So we have a couple more here, submasoteric, teregomandibular, superficial, and deep temporal

90
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:12,240
 spaces.

91
00:07:12,240 --> 00:07:16,480
 These three collectively are referred to as the masticator space.

92
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:23,080
 We have parapharyngeal, or lateral pharyngeal, retrofaryngeal, the danger space, pre-vertebral

93
00:07:23,080 --> 00:07:24,080
 space.

94
00:07:24,080 --> 00:07:27,640
 And all four of these are considered deep spaces of the neck.

95
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:33,440
 If we go back to the little drawing that I did here, we're going to be referring back

96
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:36,600
 to that for those four spaces.

97
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:38,920
 And we also have the peri-orbital space.

98
00:07:38,920 --> 00:07:44,480
 All right, so let's go through each of these spaces one at a time and unpack what's going

99
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:46,040
 on.

100
00:07:46,040 --> 00:07:48,780
 So we're going to start with the vestibular space.

101
00:07:48,780 --> 00:07:57,160
 This is the space, the potential space that lies between the cortical bone and the mucoperiostium.

102
00:07:57,160 --> 00:08:00,320
 The cortical bone and the mucoperiostium.

103
00:08:00,320 --> 00:08:05,360
 This is the most common site for spread of dental infections that break out of alveolar

104
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:06,360
 bone.

105
00:08:06,360 --> 00:08:11,880
 Again, the path of least resistance from a specific tooth is determined by the thickness

106
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:17,640
 of bone that's overlying the site of infection and the relationship of bone perforation to

107
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:19,500
 muscle attachments.

108
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:27,480
 So if a tooth, for example, this one on the left is very upright and its root apex is

109
00:08:27,480 --> 00:08:33,160
 located closer to the buccal cortical plate, there's a good chance that infection isn't

110
00:08:33,160 --> 00:08:38,400
 going to want to go through all of this bone, but rather take the easier way out and travel

111
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:40,760
 through that thinner cortex.

112
00:08:40,760 --> 00:08:48,680
 And so this is an example of a vestibular space infection on the facial side.

113
00:08:48,680 --> 00:08:56,000
 This one, this tooth that's more proclined, has a less resistance going palatally.

114
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:02,800
 So it travels, again, the path of least resistance, the bone is less thick in that direction.

115
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:08,520
 The muscle attachments, like the mentalis or other muscles that we're going to be talking

116
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:15,720
 about later, can contribute to limiting that spread of infection and creating boundaries

117
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:22,400
 for that infection that make it harder to cross over.

118
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:29,280
 The canine or infraorbital space lies between the levator, anguli, orus muscle and the levator

119
00:09:29,280 --> 00:09:36,320
 lebi superioris muscles, these two levator, I should say, levator muscles are muscles

120
00:09:36,320 --> 00:09:38,720
 of facial expression as well.

121
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:45,160
 So it lies between those muscles superficial to the canine fossa of the maxilla.

122
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:52,800
 So we're looking right at this region right here below the eye in that canine space.

123
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:58,920
 Infection presents with swelling of the vestibule, the upper lip, and even the lower eyelid

124
00:09:58,920 --> 00:10:02,120
 could get involved here if that swelling really progresses.

125
00:10:02,120 --> 00:10:08,960
 The usual dental source, the odontogenic origin for a canine space infection, is a maxillary

126
00:10:08,960 --> 00:10:15,760
 canine or a maxillary tizer, can also contribute to infection of this space.

127
00:10:15,760 --> 00:10:20,400
 Again, path of least resistance, those root apices are pretty close to this potential

128
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:23,200
 facial space.

129
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:29,760
 The periorbital space lies between the orbicularis orus muscle and the orbital septum, and so

130
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:32,800
 it's right here in that orbit.

131
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:36,800
 And the infection presents with redness and swelling of the eyelid and it may actually

132
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:40,080
 obstruct vision, which is when it gets very, very serious.

133
00:10:40,080 --> 00:10:45,200
 The path of least resistance is going to be from that canine space.

134
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:52,840
 So the canine space is a primary space, can be infected straight from one of these teeth.

135
00:10:52,840 --> 00:11:00,720
 Periorbital is a secondary space that can get infected via the canine space.

136
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:07,960
 The buckle space lies between the buxinator muscle, which is right here, and the skin.

137
00:11:07,960 --> 00:11:13,000
 And this is also the location of the buckle fat pad.

138
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:17,440
 Infection presents with marked cheek swelling, so one of their cheeks is just going to be

139
00:11:17,440 --> 00:11:23,840
 way enlarged, and especially if it's been around a while and this infection has had time

140
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:24,840
 to cook.

141
00:11:24,840 --> 00:11:33,040
 And the usual dental source for a buckle space infection is a premolar or a molar.

142
00:11:33,040 --> 00:11:42,280
 The sublingual space is located down here and it's located just under the tongue above

143
00:11:42,280 --> 00:11:45,800
 the myelohyoid muscle.

144
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:49,640
 It's also the location of that sublingual gland.

145
00:11:49,640 --> 00:11:55,320
 And swelling for this one would be evident in the floor of the mouth.

146
00:11:55,320 --> 00:12:01,800
 The usual dental source for a sublingual space infection is a mandibular incisor, canine,

147
00:12:01,800 --> 00:12:04,880
 premolar, or first molar.

148
00:12:04,880 --> 00:12:11,920
 What's really important here is that all of these teeth have roots above the attachment

149
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:15,960
 of the myelohyoid muscle to the myelohyoid line.

150
00:12:15,960 --> 00:12:21,400
 So usually this is the case that incisor canine premolar, sometimes that first molar

151
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:27,240
 might not be, have its roots above the attachment of the myelohyoid muscle.

152
00:12:27,240 --> 00:12:32,760
 But the most important thing here is that again, like we talked about before, muscles

153
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:38,960
 and muscle attachments, like this one here, create a boundary that makes infection very,

154
00:12:38,960 --> 00:12:41,920
 very hard to cross through.

155
00:12:41,920 --> 00:12:49,900
 So if, let's say, this canine root extends here and the apex is where the infection

156
00:12:49,900 --> 00:12:57,920
 is, it can pierce through the cortical plate on the lingual surface and spread into that

157
00:12:57,920 --> 00:12:59,800
 sublingual space.

158
00:12:59,800 --> 00:13:07,360
 But if this, let's say, second molar had a root apex that went down below the myelohyoid

159
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:08,360
 muscle.

160
00:13:08,360 --> 00:13:14,760
 Well, now we're talking about a different space altogether that happens below the myelohyoid

161
00:13:14,760 --> 00:13:16,320
 muscle.

162
00:13:16,320 --> 00:13:22,080
 So because of the location of those roots and the myelohyoid muscle attachment, that

163
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:30,060
 is why swelling is evident in the floor of the mouth and also why the dental infection

164
00:13:30,060 --> 00:13:37,640
 would most likely spread to the point above this myelohyoid muscle in the sublingual space.

165
00:13:37,640 --> 00:13:44,600
 Now if we go to the submandibular space, swelling would be evident at the submandibular triangle.

166
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:49,280
 And the usual dental source for this one is the mandibular second or third molar whose

167
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:54,160
 roots are below the attachment of the myelohyoid muscle to that line.

168
00:13:54,160 --> 00:13:56,960
 So this is the example that I was just proposing.

169
00:13:56,960 --> 00:14:02,920
 If you have long roots of a second molar or a third molar, the infection would pierce

170
00:14:02,920 --> 00:14:10,940
 through the lingual cortical plate into the submandibular space located below the myelohyoid

171
00:14:10,940 --> 00:14:13,040
 muscle.

172
00:14:13,040 --> 00:14:15,320
 Lastly we have the submental space.

173
00:14:15,320 --> 00:14:22,120
 This would be evident at the chin below the chin between the anterior bellies of the digastric.

174
00:14:22,120 --> 00:14:24,800
 That's where the submental triangle is.

175
00:14:24,800 --> 00:14:29,560
 The usual dental source for this one is a mandibular incisor.

176
00:14:29,560 --> 00:14:36,960
 This one would be a longer root mandibular incisor.

177
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:43,920
 So Ludwig's angina is an important clinical manifestation to talk about because it can

178
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:50,560
 certainly appear on the board exam as an aggressive form of cellulitis.

179
00:14:50,560 --> 00:14:56,680
 That's a diffuse spread of infection through phasal planes of soft tissue.

180
00:14:56,680 --> 00:15:04,000
 The Ludwig's angina involves swelling in all three of the perimandibular spaces.

181
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:08,960
 The ones we just talked about, sublingual, submandibular, and submental.

182
00:15:08,960 --> 00:15:17,200
 This swelling in all three of those perimandibular spaces elevates the floor of the mouse, which

183
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:23,640
 displaces the tongue upwards and backwards, which then blocks the oropharynx, which then

184
00:15:23,640 --> 00:15:26,400
 compromises the airway.

185
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:32,720
 So it's this domino series of events that contributes ultimately to airway compromise,

186
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:38,000
 which is, of course, very, very serious.

187
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:45,640
 Now it's going to present clinically as an underrated, a hard, massive bilateral swelling

188
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:48,240
 of the chin and the neck.

189
00:15:48,240 --> 00:15:56,080
 It can also spread to the mediastinum, which is a cavity that houses some very important

190
00:15:56,080 --> 00:16:02,200
 things like the heart and it can get there via phascial spaces of the neck, which we're

191
00:16:02,200 --> 00:16:03,960
 going to talk about in a little bit.

192
00:16:03,960 --> 00:16:11,280
 And of course that can be very, very serious for other reasons.

193
00:16:11,280 --> 00:16:16,960
 So the superficial and deep temporal spaces result, if you have an infection of those

194
00:16:16,960 --> 00:16:22,640
 spaces, it's going to result as this lateral hourglass facial swelling.

195
00:16:22,640 --> 00:16:30,480
 So this is what they're calling an hourglass shape, where you have two parts that stick

196
00:16:30,480 --> 00:16:31,480
 out.

197
00:16:31,480 --> 00:16:42,360
 So you're getting this expansion at the temple and also the cheek, and around the zygomatic

198
00:16:42,360 --> 00:16:45,160
 arch area that's staying constricted.

199
00:16:45,160 --> 00:16:51,000
 The usual dental source for an infection of this space is a maxillary second or third

200
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:54,440
 molar.

201
00:16:54,440 --> 00:16:59,560
 The sub-masoteric, also called the masseteric space, lies between the masseter and the

202
00:16:59,560 --> 00:17:00,860
 ramus.

203
00:17:00,860 --> 00:17:07,360
 And infection with this in this space frequently involves trismus, this lock jaw you're having

204
00:17:07,360 --> 00:17:11,480
 not able to open all the way or at all.

205
00:17:11,480 --> 00:17:17,920
 And the usual dental source is an impacted third molar.

206
00:17:17,920 --> 00:17:23,360
 The last mass-decator space we have to talk about is the Terego mandibular space, and

207
00:17:23,360 --> 00:17:27,560
 this one lies between the medial pterygoid and mandibular ramus.

208
00:17:27,560 --> 00:17:33,080
 It contains the sphenomandibular ligament, inferior alveolar nerve and vessels, lingular

209
00:17:33,080 --> 00:17:35,320
 nerve and the cordotimpany.

210
00:17:35,320 --> 00:17:41,200
 It's also the space that's accessed from the oral cavity through the buccinator muscle

211
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:44,840
 for inferior alveolar nerve blocks.

212
00:17:44,840 --> 00:17:49,920
 The usual dental source is a mandibular third molar that's infected.

213
00:17:49,920 --> 00:17:56,640
 This could be from a cavity, it could be from pericornitis, or a contaminated needle involved

214
00:17:56,640 --> 00:18:01,880
 in an IAN block that contaminates this space.

215
00:18:01,880 --> 00:18:08,360
 This is a high-risk infection due to the close proximity to the lateral pharyngeal space.

216
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:16,680
 This is one of those deep neck spaces that gets more and more dangerous as we go back.

217
00:18:16,680 --> 00:18:24,040
 So the mass-decator spaces are the last three we talked about, the temporal spaces, the masseteric

218
00:18:24,040 --> 00:18:28,320
 spaces and the Terego mandibular space.

219
00:18:28,320 --> 00:18:34,280
 They're called mass-decator space collectively because they all involve the muscles of mass

220
00:18:34,280 --> 00:18:44,240
-decation, and here is a drawing of those muscles and the spaces that they create boundaries

221
00:18:44,240 --> 00:18:45,320
 for.

222
00:18:45,320 --> 00:18:51,840
 And here, just like in my previous videos in this series, I have simplified it into a diamond

223
00:18:51,840 --> 00:18:57,880
 with all of the information that you need to know to be able to reproduce this in your

224
00:18:57,880 --> 00:19:03,520
 head for any kind of test question you might get concerning the mass-decator space.

225
00:19:03,520 --> 00:19:06,160
 So let me explain how I've laid this out.

226
00:19:06,160 --> 00:19:13,040
 I basically have simplified the shapes so that you can make it a lot easier to remember,

227
00:19:13,040 --> 00:19:19,160
 and I guess I'll start with the four muscles of mass-decation.

228
00:19:19,160 --> 00:19:26,880
 We have the temporalis muscle, which runs up and down like this.

229
00:19:26,880 --> 00:19:34,400
 We have the lateral pterygoid muscle, which runs side to side, and I'm just highlighting

230
00:19:34,400 --> 00:19:37,080
 these muscles here.

231
00:19:37,080 --> 00:19:46,000
 And those muscles attach to the mandible, which is running up like this, and I want

232
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:55,000
 to clarify, we're looking at the left side of the face looking from behind a person.

233
00:19:55,000 --> 00:20:01,240
 So if we cut this skull in half, we're basically looking at a cross-section from the back of

234
00:20:01,240 --> 00:20:06,760
 the head at the left side of their skull.

235
00:20:06,760 --> 00:20:11,400
 So the temporalis is inserting into the cordonoid process.

236
00:20:11,400 --> 00:20:16,600
 The lateral pterygoid is inserting into the condyle up at the top of the mandible.

237
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:20,120
 And we're going to talk about this in a future video.

238
00:20:20,120 --> 00:20:25,980
 And we also have to round out the center of this diamond, the zygomatic arch running

239
00:20:25,980 --> 00:20:28,560
 laterally like this.

240
00:20:28,560 --> 00:20:33,960
 We have the masseter muscle that goes from zygomatic arch to the bottom of the mandible

241
00:20:33,960 --> 00:20:34,960
 on the lateral side.

242
00:20:34,960 --> 00:20:39,880
 It attaches to the lateral surface of the mandibular angle.

243
00:20:39,880 --> 00:20:45,920
 The medial pterygoid runs and attaches to the medial angle of the mandible.

244
00:20:45,920 --> 00:20:50,760
 So it creates that nice little diamond shape like this.

245
00:20:50,760 --> 00:20:55,920
 And then for the last few bounds, we just have the skin of the face on the outside and the

246
00:20:55,920 --> 00:20:59,360
 skull on the inside.

247
00:20:59,360 --> 00:21:06,480
 Once we have all of that completed, now we have a really nice diamond that we just can

248
00:21:06,480 --> 00:21:08,760
 fill in those four spaces.

249
00:21:08,760 --> 00:21:15,520
 I'm going to change this color to orange to match my other, the bones that I've colored

250
00:21:15,520 --> 00:21:19,600
 in an orange.

251
00:21:19,600 --> 00:21:23,400
 So now all we have to do is fill in these four spaces.

252
00:21:23,400 --> 00:21:29,840
 We have the superficial temporal space over here that's superficial to the temporalis

253
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:31,000
 muscle.

254
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:36,080
 And then we have the deep temporal space, which is deep to the temporalis muscle.

255
00:21:36,080 --> 00:21:44,400
 We also have this little subsection of the deep temporal space located below the temporal

256
00:21:44,400 --> 00:21:49,600
 bone, and that's called the infrared temporal space.

257
00:21:49,600 --> 00:21:53,760
 That's again, that's about where we are talking about the infrared temporal fossa.

258
00:21:53,760 --> 00:21:57,480
 That's where we were in the last video.

259
00:21:57,480 --> 00:22:04,040
 And then we have the sub-massoteric space that's below underneath the masseter muscle.

260
00:22:04,040 --> 00:22:09,160
 And then we have the pterygoid mandibular space, which get ready for this one, is located

261
00:22:09,160 --> 00:22:14,800
 between the medial pterygoid muscle and the mandible.

262
00:22:14,800 --> 00:22:18,560
 And so all of those names actually make perfect sense.

263
00:22:18,560 --> 00:22:24,040
 And maybe they didn't before, but hopefully now you're able to see that, yeah, those names

264
00:22:24,040 --> 00:22:29,800
 really do make sense and they correspond to their location within the mouth.

265
00:22:29,800 --> 00:22:33,800
 So that is a nice simple way of looking at the massicular space.

266
00:22:33,800 --> 00:22:39,760
 All right, so now we're going to go to the deep spaces of the neck.

267
00:22:39,760 --> 00:22:45,120
 The paraffaryngeal or lateral pharyngeal space, these are synonymous terms, lies between the

268
00:22:45,120 --> 00:22:50,780
 masticator space anteriorly and the retrofaryngeal space posteriorly.

269
00:22:50,780 --> 00:22:57,000
 So it's located between the masticator and between the retrofaryngeal that we'll talk

270
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:58,000
 about next.

271
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:01,800
 So we're right in this region.

272
00:23:01,800 --> 00:23:07,040
 The infection of this space usually presents with swelling of a tontal and the lateral

273
00:23:07,040 --> 00:23:10,240
 pharyngeal wall on the affected side.

274
00:23:10,240 --> 00:23:16,160
 And usually you have very, very painful swallowing with this infection of this space.

275
00:23:16,160 --> 00:23:20,440
 The usual dental source is a mandibular third molar.

276
00:23:20,440 --> 00:23:26,640
 It's a very high risk space since it's very close to the retrofaryngeal space.

277
00:23:26,640 --> 00:23:31,800
 Not only that, it's continuous with it, so there's no border technically between the

278
00:23:31,800 --> 00:23:35,700
 lateral and the retrofaryngeal spaces.

279
00:23:35,700 --> 00:23:37,680
 So let's move to that one next.

280
00:23:37,680 --> 00:23:42,400
 Now we're talking about this space back here at the back of the throat and it lies between

281
00:23:42,400 --> 00:23:46,720
 the bucco-faryngeal fascia and the aller fascia.

282
00:23:46,720 --> 00:23:56,160
 So if I go back to our drawing here, now we're talking about for the retrofaryngeal space,

283
00:23:56,160 --> 00:24:01,040
 we're talking about, let me use this red color.

284
00:24:01,040 --> 00:24:04,560
 We're talking about this space right in here.

285
00:24:04,560 --> 00:24:10,480
 So it's located between the bucco-faryngeal fascia and this one which is called the aller

286
00:24:10,480 --> 00:24:11,480
 fascia.

287
00:24:11,480 --> 00:24:20,040
 Okay, so we are in this space right now, retrofaryngeal.

288
00:24:20,040 --> 00:24:28,640
 And the retrofaryngeal space spans vertically from the base of the skull all the way down

289
00:24:28,640 --> 00:24:30,400
 to the mediastinum.

290
00:24:30,400 --> 00:24:37,400
 So it's very high risk because it is essentially a highway of infection spread from the head

291
00:24:37,400 --> 00:24:44,000
 down to the mediastinum which again houses a bunch of vital organs.

292
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:50,480
 And then we have the danger space and this one lies between the aller fascia and the

293
00:24:50,480 --> 00:24:52,720
 pre-vertebral fascia.

294
00:24:52,720 --> 00:24:57,320
 So let's go back to our deep cervical fascia.

295
00:24:57,320 --> 00:25:00,360
 The danger space is located here.

296
00:25:00,360 --> 00:25:06,200
 It's between the aller fascia here and the pre-vertebral fascia which is here.

297
00:25:06,200 --> 00:25:10,000
 The danger space and retrofaryngeal spaces are often confused with each other.

298
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:15,360
 I've seen a bunch of sources online that confuse these all the time and I want to make sure

299
00:25:15,360 --> 00:25:19,400
 make this clear to you guys that these are two separate spaces.

300
00:25:19,400 --> 00:25:27,200
 The danger space spans from the base of the skull to the posterior mediastinum and diaphragm.

301
00:25:27,200 --> 00:25:33,840
 So it actually goes even further down into the body and this is an extremely high risk

302
00:25:33,840 --> 00:25:41,480
 due to just a highway of infection spread to a really important part of the body.

303
00:25:41,480 --> 00:25:49,480
 So that's an extremely, extremely serious infection if you have infection of that space.

304
00:25:49,480 --> 00:25:52,280
 And the last one I wanted to talk about is the parotid space.

305
00:25:52,280 --> 00:25:55,880
 I haven't really seen this one come up on board exams too often.

306
00:25:55,880 --> 00:26:01,000
 Infection here presents with medial bulge of the lateral pharyngeal wall, pain and that

307
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:02,160
 lock jaw.

308
00:26:02,160 --> 00:26:06,200
 It's usually not involved in the spread of dental infections.

309
00:26:06,200 --> 00:26:17,320
 So usually not an odontogenic origin here but it's rather caused by a paratitis, cellulolithiasis

310
00:26:17,320 --> 00:26:19,760
 or showgrine syndrome.

311
00:26:19,760 --> 00:26:27,440
 So it's usually non-odontogenic in origin.

312
00:26:27,440 --> 00:26:34,040
 And treatment for a facile space infection could range based on its severity as well as

313
00:26:34,040 --> 00:26:35,760
 its source.

314
00:26:35,760 --> 00:26:41,560
 So if it's an odontogenic origin we can think about extraction or pulpectomy.

315
00:26:41,560 --> 00:26:47,360
 If there is a large swelling we can think about incision and drainage to decrease the

316
00:26:47,360 --> 00:26:53,160
 bacterial load, to relieve the pressure and the pain and to increase blood flow to the

317
00:26:53,160 --> 00:26:55,400
 site to flush it out.

318
00:26:55,400 --> 00:26:58,280
 We can also think about antibiotic coverage.

319
00:26:58,280 --> 00:27:05,600
 This would be if we have diffuse swelling, if the facile spaces are involved, if there

320
00:27:05,600 --> 00:27:14,280
 is systemic manifestations like fever, tachycardia, lymphadenopathy, then antibiotic coverage

321
00:27:14,280 --> 00:27:19,360
 can certainly be considered.

322
